🐛 Describe the bug
The following code breaks when compiled:
import torch

class MyClass:
    foo: int = 1

@torch.compile(fullgraph=True)
def func(x, m):
    # return x + getattr(MyClass, "foo", 0)
    if getattr(type(m), "foo", 0):
        return x + MyClass.foo
    return x

m = MyClass()
func(torch.zeros(()), m)
func(torch.zeros(()), 0)
The error reads:
  File "/Users/vmoens/venv/rl/lib/python3.11/site-packages/torch/_dynamo/variables/builtin.py", line 1534, in call_getattr
    hasattr_var = self.call_hasattr(tx, obj, name_var)
                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  File "/Users/vmoens/venv/rl/lib/python3.11/site-packages/torch/_dynamo/variables/builtin.py", line 1438, in call_hasattr
    return obj.call_hasattr(tx, name)
           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
torch._dynamo.exc.InternalTorchDynamoError: BuiltinVariable.call_hasattr() missing 1 required positional argument: 'attr'

presumably because call_hasattr has different signatures in BuiltinVariable and other variables such as UserDefined.
The culprit is here:



      pytorch/torch/_dynamo/variables/builtin.py


        Lines 1433 to 1436
      in
      ba3b05f







           def call_hasattr(self, tx, obj, attr): 




               if attr.is_python_constant(): 




                   name = attr.as_python_constant() 




                   return obj.call_hasattr(tx, name) 





This seems to be provide a fix:
    def call_hasattr(self, tx, obj, attr):
        if attr.is_python_constant():
            name = attr.as_python_constant()
            if isinstance(obj, BuiltinVariable):
                return super(type(obj), obj).call_hasattr(tx, attr)
            return obj.call_hasattr(tx, name)
